solution_steps:
A dilation centered at the origin with a scale factor of $-3$ will stretch or shrink all points in the plane towards or away from the origin by a factor of $|-3|=3$.
Since the scale factor is negative, the dilation will also result in a reflection across the origin.
To represent this transformation as a matrix, we can write \[
\begin{pmatrix} x' \\ y' \end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix} -3 & 0 \\ 0 & -3 \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix},
\] where $(x, y)$ represents the coordinates of a point in the original plane and $(x', y')$ represents the coordinates of the corresponding point in the transformed plane.
Thus, the matrix that represents the dilation is $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} -3 & 0 \\ 0 & -3 \end{pmatrix}}$.The answer is: \begin{pmatrix}-3&0\0&-3\end{pmatrix}

