solution_steps:
Note that $\cos^2 x = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2} \cos 2x$ for all $x$.
Thus, the sum becomes
\begin{align*}
\cos^2 0^\circ + \cos^2 1^\circ + \cos^2 2^\circ + \dots + \cos^2 90^\circ &= \left( \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2} \cos 0^\circ \right) + \left( \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2} \cos 2^\circ \right) + \left( \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2} \cos 4^\circ \right) + \dots + \left( \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2} \cos 180^\circ \right) \\
&= \frac{1}{2} \cdot 91 + \frac{1}{2} \left( \cos 2^\circ + \cos 4^\circ + \dots + \cos 180^\circ \right) \\
&= \frac{1}{2} \cdot 91 + \frac{1}{2} \cdot 0 \\
&= \boxed{\frac{91}{2}}.
\end{align*}
The answer is: \frac{91}{2}

