To convert $\frac{5}{33}$ to a decimal, we perform long division:
$$
\begin{array}{c|ll}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{.} & 15151515\ldots \\
\cline{2-3}
33 & 5.00000000 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{4} & \\
\cline{2-2}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{1.00} & \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0.99} & \\
\cline{2-2}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{1.01} & \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0.99} & \\
\cline{2-2}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0.02} & \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0.00} & \\
\cline{2-2}
\end{array}
$$
We see that the decimal representation of $\frac{5}{33}$ is $0.\overline{15}$,
which means that the digits 15 repeat indefinitely.
Since the pattern 15 repeats every 2 digits, the digit in the 92nd place to the right of the decimal point is the same as the digit in the 2nd place, which is $\boxed{5}$.
The answer is: 5

