To convert a base-10 integer to base 2, we repeatedly divide by 2 and record the remainders.
For the number 200, we have the following division and remainders:
$$\begin{array}{c|c}
\multicolumn{1}{r}{2} & 200 \\
\cline{2-2}
2 & 100 \\
\cline{2-2}
0 & 50 \\
\cline{2-2}
1 & 25 \\
\cline{2-2}
1 & 12 \\
\cline{2-2}
0 & 6 \\
\cline{2-2}
1 & 3 \\
\cline{2-2}
1 & 1 \\
\cline{2-2}
\end{array}$$
Reading the remainders from bottom to top, we have $200_{10}=11001000_2$.

For the number 1200, we have the following division and remainders:
$$\begin{array}{c|c}
\multicolumn{1}{r}{2} & 1200 \\
\cline{2-2}
0 & 600 \\
\cline{2-2}
0 & 300 \\
\cline{2-2}
1 & 150 \\
\cline{2-2}
0 & 75 \\
\cline{2-2}
1 & 37 \\
\cline{2-2}
0 & 18 \\
\cline{2-2}
1 & 9 \\
\cline{2-2}
0 & 4 \\
\cline{2-2}
0 & 2 \\
\cline{2-2}
1 & 1 \\
\cline{2-2}
\end{array}$$
Reading the remainders from bottom to top, we have $1200_{10}=10010110000_2$.

Comparing the two base-2 representations, we can see that 1200 has $\boxed{3}$ additional digits compared to 200.
The answer is: 3

If the area of a square is 325 square meters, then the length of each side is $\sqrt{325}=5\sqrt{13}$
The perimeter of a square is four times the length of one side, so the perimeter of this square is $4(5\sqrt{13})=\boxed{20\sqrt{13}}$ meters.
The answer is: 20\sqrt{13}

